ct courts, that signals administrative compensation is handling emergent risks adequately and courts can focus on the truly unprecedented cases that benefit from adversarial process. The Longshore and Harbor Workers' Compensation Act already implements this election model—workers can choose administrative compensation for covered injuries or sue in tort for injuries outside the statutory schedule—and data from the Department of Labor shows that fewer than eight percent of claims proceed to litigation, suggesting that well-designed administrative categories can capture most real-world harms while preserving judicial review for edge cases. My argument assumes that AI harms will follow a similar distribution where most injuries cluster into recognizable patterns even if specific technical details vary, and that automated incident tracking can identify these clusters faster than human rulemakers working alone. If that assumption fails—if every AI harm is genuinely sui generis with no pattern recognition possible—then neither administrative compensation nor litigation reform will work efficiently, and we face a deeper governance crisis that no procedural mechanism can solve.",
